    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Tyler Soderstrom** leads the charge with an elite 76.13 efficiency score, bolstered by a prime matchup against Kumar Rocker. The combination of an advantageous hitting park and the #2 spot in the order maximizes his run-production potential. Following closely is **Mickey Moniak** (69.49), whose score is significantly elevated by a strong platoon advantage against a right-handed starter and favorable wind conditions that favor his power profile. **Seiya Suzuki** (66.61) also remains a mandatory start; he faces a vulnerable lefty in Liberatore, allowing him to capitalize on his consistent ability to drive the ball from the two-hole.\n\nWhile **Josh Naylor** (63.65) and **Austin Riley** (62.71) provide strong, reliable floors, we are navigating some volatility with our middle infield and catcher spots. **Gabriel Moreno** faces the toughest test of the day against an elite arm in Chris Sale, dropping his efficiency to 43.65; we are effectively playing him for volume rather than ceiling here. The most notable bench decision involved **Cole Young**, whose 50.03 score was respectable but ultimately squeezed out by the concentration of high-upside plays in the outfield and the positional versatility of **Isaac Paredes**, who earned the final UTIL spot by successfully leveraging a platoon edge against George Kirby.\n\nRisk management remains our priority as we await official lineups for **Josh Naylor**, **Max Muncy**, **Brice Turang**, **Mickey Moniak**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Henry Bolte**. While **Brice Turang** holds a solid 55.41 rating, he faces a daunting matchup against Yoshinobu Yamamoto; however, his 40% stolen base expectation keeps him firmly in our plans despite the elite opposing starter. We remain thin on bench depth due to the ongoing IL stints for **James Wood** and **Vinnie Pasquantino**, placing extra pressure on our active starters to maintain our efficiency targets without the safety net of high-tier replacements like **Zach McKinstry** or **Edwin Arroyo**. Keep a close watch on **Corbin Carroll**\u2014his matchup against Sale limits his upside today, but his dynamic speed remains a necessity in our quest to close the gap in the stolen base category.",
